\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Existence and uniqueness of induced volume}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Every finite-dimensional real inner-product space #{H} has the
\vocabk{induced Euclidean measure}{fdip-002M}. It is unique and
translation invariant. The associated measure on #{a+H} is independent
of the chosen origin #{a}. The zero space has unit mass at zero.
For a proper linear subspace #{H\subsetneq X_V}, the induced measure
is different from the restriction of ambient coordinate Lebesgue measure:
#{\lambda_V(H)=0}, whereas an orthonormal unit cube in #{H} has
induced mass #{1}.}
\proof{
\p{A finite Gram-Schmidt construction produces an orthonormal basis:
normalize a nonzero vector by its positive norm, subtract its components
from a spanning family, and continue in the orthogonal complement.
Positivity permits normalization at each nonzero step. In dimension zero
use the empty basis. An orthonormal basis supplies a linear isometric
isomorphism #{C:H\to\mathbb R^k}, where #{k=\dim H}. Both directions
are continuous and Borel measurable. Put
#{\mu=(C^{-1})_*\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}}.}
\p{If #{D:H\to\mathbb R^k} is any other orthonormal chart, then
#{DC^{-1}} is a linear isometric isomorphism of counting coordinate
spaces. By \ref{fdip-002L} and composition of pushforwards,
##{D_*\mu=(DC^{-1})_*\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}
 =\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}.}
Thus #{\mu} meets the every-chart characterization. Conversely, if a
Borel measure #{\nu} meets it, then #{C_*\nu=\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}}
for this one chart, and
##{\nu=(C^{-1})_*C_*\nu=(C^{-1})_*\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}=\mu.}
This proves existence and uniqueness without any assumption of finite
total mass or completed sigma-algebras. For #{k=0}, the coordinate
measure is unit mass on the singleton, and so is its inverse pushforward.}
\p{Translation by #{u\in H} becomes translation by #{Cu} in the
chart #{C}. The translation statement in \ref{fdip-002K} therefore
makes it measure preserving on #{H}. If #{a'=a+u} is another origin
in the same affine space, then #{\tau_{a'}=\tau_a\circ\tau_u};
composition of pushforwards proves independence of the origin.}
\p{For #{H\subsetneq X_V}, extend its orthonormal basis to one of
#{X_V} by the same construction. In the resulting coordinates #{H}
is a coordinate subspace and hence is closed. Since it is proper, it lies
in a coordinate hyperplane. Within each bounded cube,
that hyperplane has measure zero: the rectangle formula has a
zero-measure singleton factor and finite other factors. Monotonicity
and countable exhaustion give zero ambient measure, and
\ref{fdip-002L} transports this back. The inverse image in #{H} of
its orthonormal unit coordinate cube has induced mass #{1}, including
the singleton cube in dimension zero.}
}
\p{This deduction uses \ref{fdip-002L} and
the transformation convention of
\citet{Exercise 1.2.21, p. 41, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
Mathlib's \leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr} targets
EuclideanSpace; \leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_ofLp} and
\leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_toLp} identify its real finite PiLp
volume with ordinary coordinate products. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{AffineIsometryEquiv.measurePreserving} applies to
finite-dimensional real Borel inner-product spaces. These compare the
same canonical-volume convention.}
